  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1254 PM CDT Sat May 23 2026\n\nEarly Monday evening, there's a low (15%) chance of a few \nshowers/storms generally in the southern Big Country/Northern Concho \nValley/Heartland according to the HRRR and NAM Nest, with any \nprecipitation ending by mid evening. Monday, a brief upper level \nridge moving into the region will likely suppress any \nthunderstorm activity. Highs will remain below normal for this \ntime of year, with highs in the mid to upper 80s.\n\nTuesday, an upper level low moving into the Four Corners will result \nin moisture advection as precipitable water increases to 1.2-1.4 \ninches. Meanwhile, enhanced lift from a shortwave rotating in around \nthe Four Corners region low will combine with the moisture to bring \nanother round of scattered to widespread showers and thunderstorms, \nmainly during the afternoon through late night hours Tuesday. Right \nnow, NBM shows most likely rainfall amounts of 0.40-0.8 inches on \nTuesday, with a low chance (10%) of rainfall amounts of 1-2 inches. \nStorm motion looks somewhat weak (850-300 mb average wind of only 15-\n25 kt), so that would result in slower storm motions which would \nincrease the risk of heavy rainfall. The Weather Prediction Center \nhas issued a Marginal to Slight risk of excessive rainfall for \nTuesday. Shear looks somewhat weak, so the severe thunderstorm \nrisk looks low at this time. With the increased cloud cover and \nslight weakening of the 850 mb thermal ridge, expect slightly \ncooler temperatures Tuesday with highs in the low to mid 80s. \nAnother upper level shortwave trough will come up in southwest \nflow aloft on Wednesday and likely cause some thunderstorm \nactivity along and east of the dry-line Wednesday afternoon and \nearly evening. Highs will stay cooler than normal, in the low to \nmid 80s.\n\nThursday, a building upper level ridge should suppress thunderstorm \nactivity once again, while a strengthening 850 mb thermal ridge will \nbring a warmup with highs in the mid 80s to low 90s. Then on Friday, \nit looks like another short-wave trough will come up into southwest \nflow aloft, causing dry-line thunderstorms Friday afternoon/evening. \nA continued warmer air mass will result in highs in the upper 80s to \nlow 90s Friday. For today through next week, overnight lows will \ncontinue to be in the 60s, as the dryline looks to stay to our \nwest, keeping higher dewpoints in our area.",
